  "narrative": "The 2025 Atlanta Braves never found firm footing, stumbling through a season defined by bullpen implosions, flashes of individual brilliance, and the quiet frustration of a roster that scored enough to contend but allowed just enough more to fall short. From the very first days of April, closer Raisel Iglesias became the symbol of everything that went wrong, surrendering lead after lead in the late innings and setting a tone of heartbreak that would echo all summer long.\n\nThrough the spring, moments of hope appeared just often enough to keep faith alive. Michael Harris II emerged as the team's most reliable clutch performer, delivering timely doubles and singles when the Braves desperately needed them. Sean Murphy quietly built into one of the lineup's most dangerous threats, and Drake Baldwin provided unexpected contributions off the bench and behind the plate. But for every spark the offense generated, the bullpen — and Iglesias in particular — found a way to douse the flames.\n\nThe summer brought no resolution. June's Pierce Johnson disaster, capped by a -0.901 WPA grand slam surrender on June 7th, was the season's single most damaging moment, a gut-punch that encapsulated the Braves' inability to protect leads when it mattered most. The lineup continued to produce — Marcell Ozuna, Murphy, and Harris took turns delivering big hits — but the pitching staff as a whole, with a 4.36 ERA and a run differential barely in the red, was never quite good enough to carry the team over the threshold.\n\nBy September, the Braves were playing out the string, relying on fringe contributors like Nacho Alvarez Jr. and Ha-Seong Kim to provide late-season highlights in meaningless games. A 76-86 record and a missed playoff berth told the full story: a team with real offensive pieces and glaring bullpen vulnerabilities, one that came tantalizingly close to respectability but never close enough to October baseball.",
